\paragraph{Format dependency.}
RE uses constrained decoding (enum constraint over valid type labels), which eliminates free-form hallucination but channels all errors into type substitution, amplifying the confusability signal. NER uses unconstrained generation, where errors scatter across missed entities, boundary misalignment, and hallucinated types, diluting the pairwise signal at batch level. This format asymmetry likely contributes to the task-dependent BCS effect (\S\ref{sec:multi_channel}).

\paragraph{Practical implications.}

\paragraph{Adjusted $R^2$ and confidence intervals.}
DocRED regressions (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}) use OLS on $n = 35$ batches (5 high-BCS + 5 low-BCS + 25 random) with one predictor. At count 16, adjusted $R^2 = 0.511$ (unadjusted: 0.525; 95\% CI via Fisher $z$-transformation: [0.27, 0.73]); at count 32, adjusted $R^2 = 0.426$ (unadjusted: 0.443; CI: [0.18, 0.67]). The small shrinkage ($\Delta R^2 \leq 0.02$) indicates the unadjusted values are not inflated by the predictor-to-sample ratio.

